## Conversation
User: "I actually want to stop managing it in this repository completely,
and the same goes for Pi. I want to remove all things that make those two
tools portable. Look for any of the symlinks that link to this directory,
and clean up any of the setup or configuration steps in the install.sh
script."

Before acting I asked three scoping questions. The answers were:
1. KEEP the mcpServers.codex entry in install.sh (Claude-side config).
2. KEEP all three Codex-referencing rules files.
3. For AGENTS_global.md: "Move this file to ~/.codex/AGENTS.md instead of
   it being a symlink" -- i.e. materialize, do not just delete.

That third answer set the pattern for the whole commit: preserve live
config on the machine, then remove it from the repo.

## Actions
1. Enumerated symlinks with `find ~ -type l` + readlink, filtering for
   paths containing "vimrc". Found 4 codex symlinks. Found ZERO pi
   symlinks -- ~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md did not exist despite install.sh
   creating it (commit 82e7932), so it had been removed at some point.
2. Copied working-tree content to real files at ~/.codex/{config.toml,
   AGENTS.md} and cp -R for the two skills dirs, then verified each with
   `diff -q` / `diff -rq`. All four reported identical.
3. `git rm -f codex_config.toml` (the -f was needed because the path was
   in an unmerged UU state), plus git rm for the rest.
4. Edited install.sh: removed the codex block, the pi block, their entries
   in the rm -rf / rm -f cleanup lists, and the now-dead mise preflight.
5. Removed the "## Pi" section from README.md, the pi-coding-agent tool
   from .mise.toml, and two dead .gitignore entries.
6. rm -rf .pi-subagents (124K of untracked scout transcripts) and pi/.
7. Verified with `mise exec -- shellcheck install.sh` (clean) and
   `bash -n install.sh` (ok).

## Errors & Mistakes
The big one: codex_config.toml SHRANK from 10.6K to 4409 bytes between my
initial analysis and the moment I materialized it. Codex rewrote the file
mid-session, pruning ~30 [projects.*] trust entries, the `notify` key, and
the [mcp_servers.node_repl] and [mcp_servers.computer-use] blocks. I only
noticed because the byte count in my verification output did not match
what I had reported to the user minutes earlier.

This was NOT data loss caused by me -- `diff -q` confirmed the copy
matched the live file exactly -- but it is a sharp lesson: a symlinked
config owned by a running app is a moving target. Re-read immediately
before any copy, and verify sizes, not just diff exit codes. The
pre-prune version is recoverable from this commit's parent.

